<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Shizugatake</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1583">1583</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Shizugatake was fought in 1583 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-biwa">Lake Biwa</a> in Omi province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibata-katsuie">Shibata Katsuie</a>, rival successors to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>. Hideyoshi made a rapid forced march to the battlefield and defeated Katsuie's commander Sakuma Morimasa; Katsuie retreated to his castle of Kitanosho, where he killed himself. The victory made Hideyoshi the leading power in Japan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-shizugatake.jpg" alt="Battle of Shizugatake" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Utagawa Toyonobu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Shizugatake.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In June 1582 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>, the most powerful daimyō in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and his eldest son and heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobutada">Oda Nobutada</a> were killed in the Honno-ji Incident after being betrayed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akechi-mitsuhide">Akechi Mitsuhide</a>. Their deaths opened a succession crisis within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-clan">Oda clan</a>, as Nobunaga's sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobukatsu">Oda Nobukatsu</a> and Oda Nobutaka quarrelled over who would succeed him. Hashiba Hideyoshi, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> was then known, summoned a council to Kiyosu Castle, which declared Nobutada's 3-year-old son Oda Hidenobu the rightful heir.</p><p>Having defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akechi-mitsuhide">Akechi Mitsuhide</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamazaki">Battle of Yamazaki</a>, and with the heir an infant, Hideyoshi was well placed to succeed Nobunaga in all but name. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibata-katsuie">Shibata Katsuie</a>, one of Nobunaga's trusted generals, challenged him by moving his support from Hidenobu to Nobutaka's claim to the lordship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Katsuie ordered simultaneous attacks on Shizugatake, a series of forts in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omi-province">Ōmi Province</a>, in present-day Nagahama, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiga-prefecture">Shiga Prefecture</a>, held by Hideyoshi's generals, among them Nakagawa Kiyohide and Takayama Ukon. Katsuie's nephew Sakuma Morimasa took Iwasaki-yama and killed Nakagawa, but the defences of Shizugatake held. Hearing that Hideyoshi had camped at Ōgaki with 20,000 mounted men, Katsuie ordered Sakuma to withdraw to Ōiwa. Sakuma ignored the order, reckoning that the castle would fall before a relieving army, thought to be at least three days away, could arrive.</p><p>Meanwhile the force of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeda-toshiie">Maeda Toshiie</a> on the Shibata side, posted on Mt. Shige, abruptly left the front. Hideyoshi marched his men through the night, covering nearly 50 miles in 6 hours, and joined the defenders of Tagami. His army drove Sakuma's forces into a rout, and as the troops of Fuwa Naomitsu and Kanamori Nagachika also fell back, he pursued them to Kitanosho Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/echizen-province">Echizen Province</a>. The castle fell after three days. Before it did, Katsuie killed members of his household, including his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oichi">Oichi</a>, Nobunaga's sister, set the keep on fire and committed seppuku. Sakuma was captured and beheaded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oichi">Oichi</a>'s young daughters by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azai-nagamasa">Azai Nagamasa</a> left the castle unharmed and became Hideyoshi's adoptive daughters. In June 1583, pressed by Hideyoshi and Nobukatsu, Oda Nobutaka committed suicide, leaving a death poem that cursed Hideyoshi. Hideyoshi still faced Nobukatsu, now allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-komaki-and-nagakute">Battle of Komaki and Nagakute</a> in 1584. The historian George Sansom counted Shizugatake among the decisive battles of Japanese history.</p><p>Hideyoshi's seven chief generals in the battle became known as the Seven Spears of Shizugatake (shichi-hon yari). Among them were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kato-kiyomasa">Katō Kiyomasa</a>, later one of Hideyoshi's closest retainers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukushima-masanori">Fukushima Masanori</a> and Wakizaka Yasuharu.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Shizugatake” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hideyoshi, a foot soldier's son who rose in Oda Nobunaga's service, avenged Nobunaga by defeating Akechi Mitsuhide at Yamazaki in 1582 and then beat Shibata Katsuie at <b>Shizugatake</b> in 1583.</blockquote><a href="/state/toyotomi-regime">Toyotomi regime</a></li></ul></section>
